---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.js';

const title = `About · ${SITE.name}`;
const description = SITE.description;
---

<Base {title} {description}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} answers one question per page: for the SaaS you're paying for, can you run the
      open-source equivalent yourself — and if so, exactly how. Every page offers both paths, and
      you pick: on a rented server it takes two prompts, one to get a bare machine ready and then
      one per app; on your own computer it takes one, with no server, no domain, and nothing
      exposed to the internet. {SITE.promise}
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is open source (MIT), the prompts are free forever, and the things that keep your
      data safe — backup, restore, TLS renewal, the basic upgrade — will never be paywalled.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reviewed by <strong>{SITE.author.name}</strong>. Drafting is AI-assisted; every published
      prompt must run end-to-end on a clean machine before it earns a stamp, and pages that
      haven't are labelled. The full rulebook is on{' '}
      <a href="/methodology/">/methodology</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Credit</h2>
    <p>
      Inspired by <a href={SITE.inspiredBy.url} rel="noopener">{SITE.inspiredBy.name}</a>, Rob
      Hallam's directory of what AI can and can't rebuild. Different operator, different question,
      shared conviction: honest verdicts beat listicles.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding-block: 3rem 4rem;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2em;
  }
</style>
